  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ increases on the right-hand side or decreases on the left-hand side of the graph, the function continues to increase without bound (as indicated by the two arrows). So, the function does not reach a global maximum value.\n\n- Statement II is false. The global minimum points are $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,-1).$ So the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x)=-1.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function $f(x)$ has global minimums at the points $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,-1),$ and a global minimum is a type of global extremum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"",
